Relevant tables, columns, and their potential usage  

collisions  
• collision_severity : lets us confirm the crash involved injuries or fatalities  
• motorcycle_collision : flag (1 / 0) telling whether any motorcycle was involved  
• case_id : primary key to connect to parties  

parties  
• case_id : join‐key to collisions  
• statewide_vehicle_type : identify motorcycle parties (value contains “motorcycle”)  
• party_safety_equipment_1 / party_safety_equipment_2 : determine helmet usage  
• party_number_killed : fatalities within that party (driver + passengers)  
• party_type : ‘driver’ or ‘passenger’ – we only need motorcycle riders & passengers  
Conditions likely used  
• collisions."motorcycle_collision" = 1  OR parties."statewide_vehicle_type" LIKE '%motorcycle%'  
• Helmet used  → parties."party_safety_equipment_1"  LIKE '%helmet%'  OR  parties."party_safety_equipment_2" LIKE '%helmet%'  
• Helmet NOT used → parties."party_safety_equipment_1" NOT LIKE '%helmet%' AND parties."party_safety_equipment_2" NOT LIKE '%helmet%'  
• Fatalities → parties."party_number_killed" > 0  

Below are 10 exploratory → increasingly complex SQL queries (all return ≤ 20 rows) that reveal the needed values and culminate in the final calculation.

```sql
-- 1. List distinct values that appear in statewide_vehicle_type
SELECT DISTINCT statewide_vehicle_type
FROM parties
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Inspect safety‑equipment field #1 to see how a helmet is recorded
SELECT DISTINCT party_safety_equipment_1
FROM parties
WHERE party_safety_equipment_1 IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect safety‑equipment field #2 as well
SELECT DISTINCT party_safety_equipment_2
FROM parties
WHERE party_safety_equipment_2 IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Show some motorcycle parties together with their safety gear
SELECT case_id,
       party_number,
       party_safety_equipment_1,
       party_safety_equipment_2,
       party_number_killed
FROM parties
WHERE statewide_vehicle_type LIKE '%motorcycle%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Verify the collisions table really flags motorcycle crashes
SELECT DISTINCT motorcycle_collision
FROM collisions
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Join collisions ↔ parties to make sure the two sources agree
SELECT p.case_id,
       c.motorcycle_collision,
       p.statewide_vehicle_type
FROM parties AS p
JOIN collisions AS c USING (case_id)
WHERE p.statewide_vehicle_type LIKE '%motorcycle%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Count total motorcycle collisions (one row per collision)
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT case_id) AS total_moto_collisions
FROM collisions
WHERE motorcycle_collision = 1;
```

```sql
-- 8. Count total motorcycle collisions that had ≥1 fatality when a helmet WAS worn
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT p.case_id) AS collisions_helmet
FROM parties AS p
WHERE p.statewide_vehicle_type LIKE '%motorcycle%'
  AND (p.party_safety_equipment_1  LIKE '%helmet%'
       OR p.party_safety_equipment_2 LIKE '%helmet%');
```

```sql
-- 9. Count the number of motorcyclist fatalities when a helmet WAS worn
SELECT SUM(p.party_number_killed) AS killed_helmet
FROM parties AS p
WHERE p.statewide_vehicle_type LIKE '%motorcycle%'
  AND (p.party_safety_equipment_1  LIKE '%helmet%'
       OR p.party_safety_equipment_2 LIKE '%helmet%');
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL: Compute the requested two fatality‑rate percentages
SELECT
  -- percentage when helmet worn
  100.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN helmet = 1 THEN fatalities ELSE 0 END) /
          NULLIF(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN helmet = 1 THEN case_id END),0) AS pct_fatal_helmet,
  -- percentage when helmet NOT worn
  100.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN helmet = 0 THEN fatalities ELSE 0 END) /
          NULLIF(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN helmet = 0 THEN case_id END),0) AS pct_fatal_no_helmet
FROM (
    SELECT 
        p.case_id,
        CASE 
          WHEN p.party_safety_equipment_1 LIKE '%helmet%'
            OR p.party_safety_equipment_2 LIKE '%helmet%' THEN 1
          ELSE 0
        END AS helmet,
        p.party_number_killed AS fatalities
    FROM parties AS p
    WHERE p.statewide_vehicle_type LIKE '%motorcycle%'
) AS sub;
```

Each query is capped at 20 rows (when a result set is row‑based) and uses DISTINCT where appropriate so you can iteratively inspect the data and, with the final query, obtain the two percentages required by the task.
